WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.519 --> 00:00:02.794
(symphonic music)

00:00:02.794 --> 00:00:05.607
- Snowshoes are designed
to help you maintain

00:00:05.607 --> 00:00:08.625
the weight of your body on top of the snow

00:00:08.625 --> 00:00:10.708
as opposed to sinking in.

00:00:11.938 --> 00:00:16.105
And with every pair of
snowshoes, there's a story.

00:00:23.056 --> 00:00:27.376
I'm Bill Mackowski, I’m a snowshoe
maker and a snowshoe collector.

00:00:27.376 --> 00:00:30.629
Over the years, I've
collected over 200 pair.

00:00:30.629 --> 00:00:32.573
As far as we know it's
the largest collection

00:00:32.573 --> 00:00:35.038
of snowshoes in the world.

00:00:35.038 --> 00:00:39.185
For Native Americans, snow
shoes were not a convenience,

00:00:39.185 --> 00:00:40.768
they were critical.

00:00:41.647 --> 00:00:45.101
An individual may have three or
four or five different pairs of shoes

00:00:45.101 --> 00:00:47.160
that he used for different conditions.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.767
There's a shoe for spring,
for deep fluffy snow,

00:00:49.767 --> 00:00:51.077
for straight travel.

00:00:51.077 --> 00:00:53.744
There's a wide range of designs.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.626
I first got started using
snowshoes as a young fella.

00:01:02.626 --> 00:01:04.929
I spent a good part of
my life in the woods,

00:01:04.929 --> 00:01:07.434
trapping, fishing, and hunting.

00:01:07.434 --> 00:01:10.486
In the mid-'80’s, I was
flying in northern Quebec

00:01:10.486 --> 00:01:13.280
and came upon a winter
camp and found a pair

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.599
of traditional Native snow shoes.

00:01:15.599 --> 00:01:17.652
Being a snowshoe maker
I had an appreciation

00:01:17.652 --> 00:01:21.810
for the unbelievable
craftsmanship and skill

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:24.468
that went into making these
traditional snow shoes.

00:01:24.468 --> 00:01:27.752
From that point I kind
of embarked on a quest

00:01:27.752 --> 00:01:29.988
to find some of these
other villages that had

00:01:29.988 --> 00:01:31.553
snowshoe makers.

00:01:31.553 --> 00:01:34.894
I visited every native
community in Quebec,

00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:37.543
in the Yukon, several in
the northwest territories,

00:01:37.543 --> 00:01:40.265
in Ontario, Alaska and all
up and down the north shore

00:01:40.265 --> 00:01:41.598
of St. Lawrence.

00:01:43.635 --> 00:01:47.323
What I tried to do is to ask
them about their techniques.

00:01:47.323 --> 00:01:49.092
Things like, how do you
know what kind of wood

00:01:49.092 --> 00:01:50.951
you want to use, how do you
know which tree is right for the wires,

00:01:50.951 --> 00:01:54.534
why do you have this
kind of a weave in it?

00:01:56.482 --> 00:01:58.512
(buzzes)

00:01:58.512 --> 00:02:00.679
(scrapes)

00:02:01.607 --> 00:02:05.150
In the process of all this
documenting and visiting,

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:09.715
I learned a considerable amount
about crafting shoes myself.

00:02:09.715 --> 00:02:14.051
It's given me just a
phenomenal appreciation for,

00:02:14.051 --> 00:02:17.551
not only their skill but their lifestyle.

00:02:21.526 --> 00:02:23.489
The primary focus at this point should be

00:02:23.489 --> 00:02:27.561
to get this collection in a
situation where it's available

00:02:27.561 --> 00:02:30.897
for people to study, for scholars,

00:02:30.897 --> 00:02:33.475
and for people to see.

00:02:33.475 --> 00:02:35.304
I know other people
have collected snowshoes,

00:02:35.304 --> 00:02:40.099
but I don't think anyone has
ever done it on this scale.

00:02:40.099 --> 00:02:42.633
I don't think it's anything
that could ever be done again,

00:02:42.633 --> 00:02:46.115
simply because that skill is that lost.

00:02:46.115 --> 00:02:50.282
It's really the last of the
traditional snowshoe makers.

